# M-HEC

**MLFF-Enhanced High-Temperature Elastic Constants**

M-HEC is an open-source Python package that computes single-crystal elastic
constants at finite temperature by combining VASP *ab initio* molecular dynamics
(AIMD) with on-the-fly machine-learning force fields (MLFF) and
symmetry-adapted stress-strain sampling.

- Version 1.0.0
- Author Dr. Feng Xing
- Email fengxing@amgm.ac.cn
- License MIT

---

## 1. Overview

M-HEC evaluates the isothermal elastic tensor from the stress response to finite
strains. For each temperature the equilibrium lattice is first obtained from an
NPT simulation, deformations are then sampled under fixed-cell NVT conditions,
and the time-averaged stresses are fitted to Hooke's law

```
sigma_i = C_ij * eps_j        (i, j = 1 to 6, Voigt notation)
```

The central design goal is **strain-domain consistency**. The force field is
trained and validated on the same strain range and at the same
thermal-expansion-equilibrium lattice that are later used for the elastic
measurement, so that the elastic constants are obtained by interpolation rather
than extrapolation.

Key features

- Automatic identification of the crystal system and Laue class, with optional
  space-group input. All seven crystal systems (nine Laue classes) are supported.
- Two complementary deformation schemes, SC-SS and SA-SS (Section 3).
- One symmetry-parameterised, R-squared-weighted joint least-squares solver
  shared by both schemes, which returns each independent C_ij together with a
  fit standard error.
- Robust per-channel slope fitting with automatic model selection
  (odd polynomial, linear, or small-strain window).
- Wallace residual-pressure diagnostics and the Birch to Brugger correction.
- Born mechanical-stability checking.
- MLFF training (isothermal and heating-ramp) and energy, force and stress
  parity validation.
- Post-processing of mechanical properties (VRH moduli, anisotropy,
  direction-dependent moduli, Debye temperature, sound velocities) and of AIMD
  trajectories.
- Equilibrium lattice and thermal-expansion analysis.

---

## 2. Requirements and installation

Requirements

| Component | Notes |
|---|---|
| Python | 3.8 or newer |
| numpy | required |
| VASP | 6.x recommended, for AIMD and on-the-fly MLFF |
| matplotlib | optional, for diagnostic figures |
| spglib | optional but recommended, for reliable Laue-class detection |
| pyyaml | optional, for YAML configuration files |

**Option A, from the source archive.** Unpack the distributed tarball, enter the
extracted directory and install from it. This is the recommended route.

```bash
tar -xzf mhec-1.0.0.tar.gz
cd mhec-1.0.0
pip install .
```

To include the optional plotting dependencies, use the following instead of the
last command.

```bash
pip install ".[full]"
```

If the account has no write access to the system site-packages, install into the
user tree.

```bash
pip install --user .
```

**Option B, from the wheel.** No unpacking is required.

```bash
pip install --force-reinstall --no-deps mhec-1.0.0-py3-none-any.whl
```

**Option C, from the archive without unpacking.** pip can also read the tarball
directly.

```bash
pip install mhec-1.0.0.tar.gz
```

Verify the installation

```bash
mhec --version
```

If the `mhec` command is not found after a user installation, add the user script
directory to the search path.

```bash
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
```

---

## 3. Deformation schemes

**SC-SS, Single-Component Stress-Strain.** Each deformation activates a single
Voigt strain component, so one deformation probes a full column of the stiffness
matrix. This scheme is general and applies to every crystal system.

**SA-SS, Symmetry-Adapted Stress-Strain.** Deformations are chosen to match the
symmetry of the crystal and to maximise the measured stress signal. Volume-
conserving shears are used for the shear block, and deviatoric strains are used
for combinations such as C11 minus C12. For hexagonal, tetragonal and trigonal
systems an `ortho_ac` mode with strain (delta, a, minus delta) provides a strong
differential constraint on C13 through the difference of the first and third
normal stresses.

Both schemes are processed by the same solver and produce the same output files,
so results can be compared directly.

---

## 4. Quick start

Prepare a working directory that contains `POSCAR`, `POTCAR`, `KPOINTS`,
`INCAR` and a cluster submission script `run.sh`. Then launch the interactive
interface

```bash
mhec
```

A typical sequence is

1. Item 10, confirm the crystal system and lattice parameters.
2. Item 7 or 8, train an MLFF, then item 9 to validate its accuracy.
3. Item 12, obtain the equilibrium lattice at each temperature.
4. Item 1 or 3, generate the elastic-constant inputs, and submit them.
5. Item 2 or 4, fit the elastic constants.
6. Item 5, post-process the mechanical properties.

---

## 5. Command-line interface

The numbered menu is grouped into four blocks.

**Elastic Constants**

| Item | Function |
|---|---|
| 1 | SC-SS Single-Component Stress-Strain, generate inputs |
| 2 | SC-SS Single-Component Stress-Strain, fit elastic constants |
| 3 | SA-SS Symmetry-Adapted Stress-Strain, generate inputs |
| 4 | SA-SS Symmetry-Adapted Stress-Strain, fit elastic constants |

**Post-processing**

| Item | Function |
|---|---|
| 5 | Elastic post-processing, mechanical properties |
| 6 | AIMD post-processing |

**Machine-Learning Force Field**

| Item | Function |
|---|---|
| 7 | MLFF training, isothermal |
| 8 | MLFF training, heating ramp |
| 9 | MLFF accuracy validation |

**Utilities**

| Item | Function |
|---|---|
| 10 | Crystal system and lattice parameters |
| 11 | Deformation modes and strain schemes |
| 12 | Equilibrium lattice and thermal expansion |

The header of the interface reports the version, the detected system, the
temperatures and the strain settings. Item `L` switches the interface language
and item `0` exits.

---

## 6. Batch mode and configuration

Batch mode

```bash
mhec --poscar POSCAR --temp 1000 --strain 0.005 --batch
```

Using a configuration file

```bash
mhec --config mhec.yaml --batch --poscar POSCAR
```

Command-line options

| Option | Description |
|---|---|
| `--poscar` | path to the POSCAR file |
| `--primitive` | path to a primitive-cell POSCAR, used for crystal-system detection |
| `--crystal-system` | force the crystal system, overriding automatic detection |
| `--temp` | temperature or list of temperatures in K |
| `--strain` | base strain amplitude delta |
| `--method` | advanced strain scheme, `standard` or `ulics` |
| `--batch` | non-interactive batch mode |
| `--config` | path to a YAML or JSON configuration file |
| `--version` | print the version and exit |

Example `mhec.yaml`

```yaml
temperatures: [300, 600, 900, 1200, 1500]
magnitude: 0.005
n_points: 9
elastic_method: sa        # sa = SA-SS, sc = SC-SS
fit_poly_order: 3         # 3 = odd polynomial, 1 = linear
space_group: 92           # optional, most reliable route to the Laue class
skip_steps: 500
vasp:
  encut: 600
  nsw_train: 2000
  nsw_run: 10000
  potim: 1.0
```

Strain sampling can be specified in three equivalent ways, with the priority
`strain_points`, then `max_strain` with `n_points`, then `magnitude` with
`n_points`. The number of points must be 3, 5, 7, 9 or 11. With
`magnitude = 0.005` and `n_points = 9` the non-zero strains are plus and minus
0.005, 0.010, 0.015 and 0.020.

---

## 7. Output files

| File | Content |
|---|---|
| `elastic_constants.txt` | full report, raw matrix, symmetrized Birch matrix, Wallace diagnostics, Brugger matrix, Born stability |
| `elastic.txt` | plain 6 by 6 matrix in GPa, recommended values |
| `elastic_birch.txt` | uncorrected Birch matrix, written when the pressure correction is applied |
| `fit_solution.txt` | SC-SS provenance, how each C_ij is solved, with standard errors |
| `vcfit_solution.txt` | SA-SS provenance, same format |
| `fit_*.png`, `vcfit_*.png` | stress-strain diagnostic plots for the strong channels |
| `fit_r2_heatmap.png` | R-squared heat map, weak and symmetry-zero channels greyed out |
| `fit_*_GPa.dat` | fitted data ready for external plotting |

In the provenance files each independent constant is reported as
`C_ij = value +/- standard error GPa`, followed by the strong channels that
constrain it. Channels below the R-squared threshold are listed separately and
flagged, and constants that are poorly constrained are marked so that they are
not used without further checking.

---

## 8. Method notes

**Fitting.** For each stress channel the slope at vanishing strain is obtained
from an odd polynomial through the origin, sigma equal to a times eps plus b
times eps cubed, which removes the bias caused by the leading anharmonic term.
If the odd polynomial does not improve on a linear fit it is rejected in favour
of the linear form. If the fit quality is still poor the outer strain points are
removed and the slope is taken from the largest small-strain window that reaches
the quality threshold. The choice made for every channel is recorded in the
provenance file.

**Joint solution.** All equations from all deformations are assembled into one
overdetermined system, the stiffness matrix is parameterised by the symmetry of
the crystal, and the system is solved by least squares with weights derived from
the fit quality of each channel. Uncertainties follow from the weighted residual
covariance.

**Pressure correction.** The residual hydrostatic pressure of the reference
configuration is reported. When its magnitude exceeds 0.5 GPa the Birch
coefficients are converted to Brugger coefficients, which are the quantities to
compare with experiment.

**Practical guidance.** Keep the strain amplitude inside the range used for
force-field training. For soft framework materials the linear range can be
narrow, so amplitudes of about 0.005 to 0.01 are preferable to 0.03. Except for
monatomic Bravais lattices, static stress-strain calculations must relax the
internal ions at the fixed deformed cell, otherwise the clamped-ion tensor is
obtained.

---

## 9. Plugins

Custom extensions are registered through entry points.

| Entry point | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `mhec.mechanical` | mechanical-property post-processors |
| `mhec.aimd_postprocessor` | AIMD post-processors |

